. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 * 
. * This section tests whether having daughters leads to more left-leaning poli
> tical preferences (H2). 
. * 
.                 * Dependent Variable:  
.                 * Political ideology is measured using the SOEP item plh0004,
>  which asks respondents to place themselves 
.                 * on an 11-point left–right political scale from 0 ("Far left
> ") to 10 ("Far right"). To reduce subjective 
.                 * variation in how respondents use the intermediate scale poi
> nts, we recode this variable into a binary 
.                 * indicator, d_left. This variable equals 1 if the respondent
>  reports a value between 0 and 3 (inclusive), 
.                 * indicating left-leaning political preferences, and 0 otherw
> ise. This approach focuses on individuals who 
.                 * explicitly self-identify with the political left, thereby a
> ligning with prior work (Oswald & Powdthavee, 2010) 
.                 * and emphasizing meaningful attitudinal clusters over potent
> ially arbitrary distinctions. 
.                 * 
.                 * Model Specification:  
.                 * We estimate a logistic regression to test whether having da
> ughters is associated with an increased likelihood 
.                 * of left-leaning political preferences. To isolate the effec
> t of daughters from family size effects, we include 
.                 * total number of children fixed-effects (i.tot_kids). Standa
> rd errors are clustered at the respondent level 
.                 * to account for repeated observations in the panel dataset. 
> The model is specified as follows:  
. 
.                 logit d_left daughters_nr `controls' i.tot_kids, cluster(pid)

note: 9.tot_kids != 0 predicts failure perfectly;
      9.tot_kids omitted and 2 obs not used.

note: 10.tot_kids != 0 predicts failure perfectly;
      10.tot_kids omitted and 3 obs not used.

